To meet this requirement, we use the Salesforce REST API's sObject Collections resource (`/services/data/v58.0/composite/sobjects`). 

The sObject Collections endpoint allows us to perform DML operations on multiple records in a single request, significantly reducing API calls. We use the `allOrNone` boolean property in the JSON payload to control the transaction behavior. 

1. For the **Contact creations**, we use a `POST` request with `allOrNone: true` to enforce an atomic transaction (either all contacts are inserted, or the entire operation rolls back).
2. For the **Account and Opportunity updates**, we use a `PATCH` request with `allOrNone: false` to allow partial successes. Even if updating the Account fails (e.g., due to a validation rule), the Opportunity update will still attempt to save.

```http
POST /services/data/v58.0/composite/sobjects HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "records": [
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "FirstName": "Maya",
      "LastName": "Chen",
      "Email": "maya.chen@example.com",
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "FirstName": "Luis",
      "LastName": "Ortega",
      "Email": "luis.ortega@example.com",
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "FirstName": "Priya",
      "LastName": "Nair",
      "Email": "priya.nair@example.com",
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB"
    }
  ]
}
###
PATCH /services/data/v58.0/composite/sobjects HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": false,
  "records": [
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Account" },
      "Id": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB",
      "Rating": "Hot"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Opportunity" },
      "Id": "0065g00000TrAd9AAB",
      "NextStep": "Send pricing"
    }
  ]
}
```

To fulfill these requirements (transactional boundaries per order, independent failure handling for each order, and linking via reference IDs), the best approach is to use the **Composite Graph API**. 

Each order is represented as a separate graph with its own `graphId`. A failure in one graph rolls back only that graph, while allowing the other graphs to succeed.

```http
POST /services/data/v58.0/composite/graph HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1001",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1001",
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Alder Creek Cafe"
          }
        },
        {
          "referenceId": "opp_1001",
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1001",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 1250,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1001.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1002",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1002",
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-2002",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Birch Lane Bakery"
          }
        },
        {
          "referenceId": "opp_1002",
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1002",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 980,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1002.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

To achieve this in a single all-or-nothing REST API call and ensure the Asset is saved before the `Service_Request__c` trigger fires, we will use the Composite API. 

The Composite API processes requests sequentially in the order provided, which satisfies the requirement for the Asset to exist before the Service Request is evaluated by the trigger. We link the Account to the Asset using the `@{newAccount.id}` reference syntax and make the entire transaction all-or-nothing using the `"allOrNone": true` property.

```http
POST /services/data/v50.0/composite HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "compositeRequest": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Account",
      "referenceId": "newAccount",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Asset",
      "referenceId": "newAsset",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Trailhead Kiosk 3000",
        "SerialNumber": "SN-88213",
        "AccountId": "@{newAccount.id}"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Service_Request__c",
      "referenceId": "newRequest",
      "body": {
        "Subject__c": "Installation",
        "Serial_Number__c": "SN-88213"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

To create or update a record and simultaneously link it to a parent record using an External ID, you use the HTTP `PATCH` method against the sObject External ID endpoint. The parent relationship is populated by providing a nested JSON object keyed by the relationship name (for the standard `ParentId` field, the relationship name is `Parent`).

```http
PATCH /services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/ERP_Customer_Id__c/ERP-10042 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply",
  "Phone": "+1 503 555 0199",
  "BillingCity": "Portland",
  "Parent": {
    "ERP_Customer_Id__c": "ERP-10000"
  }
}
