**What I did differently:**
I bulkified the code by first collecting all the `AccountId`s from the cases, performing a single SOQL query to get all the Accounts into a Map, and then looping through the cases again to set the priority.

**Why:**
You mentioned that the ERP integration creates cases in batches of up to 200. Salesforce has a strict governor limit of 100 synchronous SOQL queries per transaction. If you query inside the loop for a batch of 200 cases, the code will fail with a `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101` error on the 101st case, causing the entire ERP transaction to roll back. The bulkified approach guarantees this will not happen, while still remaining very readable and easy for admins to maintain.

