public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /* 
     * I would not use a nested loop here.
     * While a nested loop might seem simple, looping 3,000 leads against 3,000 contacts 
     * results in up to 9,000,000 iterations. This is highly inefficient in Apex and risks 
     * hitting the CPU time limit exception. 
     * 
     * Instead, using a Map reduces the time complexity from O(N*M) to O(N+M). It populates 
     * a map of normalized emails to Contact Ids (only storing the first match found to respect 
     * the order requirement), and then does a fast O(1) lookup for each Lead. This is the 
     * standard best practice for bulk-safe Apex.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        Map<String, Id> emailToContactId = new Map<String, Id>();

        // Build the lookup map from contacts
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Email)) {
                String normalizedEmail = c.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                // Only put the first contact found to respect the order requirement
                if (!emailToContactId.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                    emailToContactId.put(normalizedEmail, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // Match leads
        List<Id> result = new List<Id>();
        for (Lead l : leads) {
            Id matchedId = null;
            if (String.isNotBlank(l.Email)) {
                String normalizedEmail = l.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                matchedId = emailToContactId.get(normalizedEmail);
            }
            result.add(matchedId);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

To ensure the solution is bulk-safe, I changed the design from enqueuing one job per case to enqueuing a single job that processes a collection of Case IDs. Salesforce enforces a governor limit of 50 `System.enqueueJob` calls per transaction. If a nightly cleanup closes 200 cases at once, a loop enqueuing 200 jobs would crash with a `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 50`. 

Instead, the trigger gathers all eligible Case IDs and passes them as a `Set<Id>` to a single `CaseSurveyJob`, which queries the cases and inserts the tasks in bulk. If individual failure isolation is still strictly required later, the queueable could be changed to a Batch Apex class or use `Database.insert(tasks, false)`.

